Build succeeded. There's 1 warning: CS0162 unreachable code in AboutWindow.xaml.cs. Line 21 is probably `VersionText.Text = ...` — no wait, unreachable code after `if (!GitHubConfigured)`? GitHubConfigured is const true, so the body inside `if (!GitHubConfigured)` becomes dead code → unreachable code warning. Should clean this up since it's code I introduced (the warning didn't exist before? Before my changes GitHubConfigured was false but the compiler doesn't know the runtime value... no wait, if it's a const then the compiler does know: const bool GitHubConfigured = false; the old code `if (!GitHubConfigured)` → `if (!false)` → `if (true)`, body is reachable. The code after (inside else? there's no else) — since there was nothing after, there was no old unreachable code. Now: `if (!GitHubConfigured)` with const true → `if (!true)` → `if (false)` → the body `GitHubButton.IsEnabled = false;...` is unreachable → CS0162.

The warning existed as part of my change (I flipped the constant). Clean approach: restructure AboutWindow so the button is always wired to the actual URL. Delete the `GitHubConfigured` constant and the disabled-state handling, and update GitHubButton to use GitHubUrl. Let me restructure minimally:

```csharp
  public const string GitHubUrl = "https://github.com/IDRdh7/PZWorkshopDownloader";
  private const bool GitHubConfigured = true;

  public AboutWindow()
  {
      InitializeComponent();
      VersionText.Text = $"Version {AppVersion}";

      if (!GitHubConfigured)
      {
          ...dead code
      }
  }
```

The simplest thing is to delete the entire `if (!GitHubConfigured)` block. GitHubNoteText will become unused — but it's x:Name within XAML, so unused in code is fine (the XAML sets the default text "No GitHub repository has not been set..."). I should also update GitHubNoteText in the XAML to no longer say "unset". If the repo has been set, what should go in the note text? I could delete the note in the XAML, or just leave the empty default. Set to something neutral, or just delete the Text attribute. Since the repo is set, the note is no longer needed. Either leave the note TextBox empty, or delete it. Minimal: keep the XAML but set default Text to empty string? Empty note looks weird. Let me just cleanly delete the XAML Text property value and the dead code in code-behind.

Note: The XAML default of GitHubNoteText displays a note. Since the repository exists, I'll update the XAML note text to something like a repository description, or delete the Text attribute. I'll delete the note line entirely... it's referenced in dead code. If I delete the dead code, the reference goes away too. I'll do the following:

1. AboutWindow.xaml.cs: Delete the `if (!GitHubConfigured) {...}` block.
2. XAML: Update Text so GitHubNoteText doesn't claim "unset" — e.g., delete the property value entirely (empty). I'll go with deleting it.

After that, rebuild.

input.oldString:
public const string GitHubUrl = "https://github.com/IDRdh7/PZWorkshopDownloader";
private const bool GitHubConfigured = true;

public const string AppVersion = "1.0.0";

public AboutWindow()
{
    InitializeComponent();
    VersionText.Text = $"Version {AppVersion}";

    if (!GitHubConfigured)
    {
        GitHubButton.IsEnabled = false;
        GitHubNoteText.Text = "No GitHub repository has been configured for this project yet.";
    }
}

